The representation uses a fixed pattern of filled/empty circles (⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤⬤) that appears identically in every surviving copy. This pattern is most economically explained as deliberate redaction, moral squeamishness on the part of copyists, physical damage with placeholder notation, or an esoteric cue that only initiates could expand.  

Option H is the least plausible because it requires an unsupported chain of assumptions: that the circles are a corrupted transcription of the Kuji-in, that they encode “nine holes”/erotic Taoist rites specific to kunoichi, and that this hidden sexual-mystical layer was then innocently reduced to blank circles by later scribes. No contemporary source links Bansenshukai’s kunoichi passage to such practices, the Kuji-in symbols are already well-attested in other texts without erotic overlays, and the uniform circle pattern across independent copies is far more consistent with simple omission or censorship than with a garbled esoteric diagram. All other options, while varying in historical probability, at least rest on mechanisms (redaction, moral filtering, physical wear, oral mnemonics, state secrecy) that are attested elsewhere in Edo-period documentary practice; H does not.}

