5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H}

H remains the clearest outlier among the options. It requires positing an entire layer of Kuji-in reinterpretation as “nine holes,” Taoist erotic-energy work, and yin-yang perception rituals that no surviving Iga or Kōga manuscript, including Fujibayashi’s own writings, ever records. The uniform placement of the circles across independent transcriptions is far more consistent with simple editorial omission, later official redaction, or physical wear than with a chain of identical scribal misreadings of an esoteric sexual diagram.  

By comparison, C’s proposed motive—protecting the political legacy of a high-ranking Tokugawa-associated figure—aligns with documented patterns of selective censorship in early-Edo intelligence documents, even if the precise link to Saigō remains unproven. The other choices likewise rest on attested mechanisms (moral filtering by copyists, Oniwaban-style secrecy, aburidashi practices, or heavy-use degradation) rather than the wholesale invention of an anachronistic sexual-mystical framework. H alone collapses under scrutiny of the actual textual and chronological record.}

