PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H remains the clearest outlier among the options. Its chain of assumptions—an unrecorded erotic reinterpretation of the Kuji-in as “nine holes,” Taoist sexual-alchemy rituals supposedly employed by kunoichi, and a subsequent uniform reduction of those symbols to identical blank circles across independent copies—finds no support in any surviving shinobi text, in Fujibayashi’s known corpus, or in Edo-period documentary practice. The fixed pattern ⬤○○⬤⬤⬤⬤○⬤⬤⬤⬤⬤ itself argues against accidental scribal misreading, because a garbled esoteric diagram would not reproduce the same sequence in every lineage.  

Colleague 11’s chronology objection to C is worth noting, yet the argument may be overly rigid: even if Lady Saigō herself could not have been the direct subject, later copyists or domain officials might still have projected retrospective concern onto any female-espionage material and applied a uniform redaction. That possibility, while speculative, stays within the realm of attested Tokugawa-era censorship habits. By contrast, H requires an entire modern pseudo-esoteric framework that has no anchor in period sources.  

Options such as E or B, though also imperfect, at least invoke documented techniques (aburidashi) or attested social pressures on copyists and therefore remain more plausible than H’s wholesale invention.}

